	\chapter{Introduction}
	\section{Background}
	With the rise of digitization, the volume of digital news articles available in Nepal across 
	multiple channels and languages has increased tremendously. However, as content 
	continues to be digitized, filtering and categorizing news articles for readers and agencies 
	has become increasingly challenging. A large portion of this information is not explicitly 
	labeled as “news,” and it is often scattered across different sources, making it difficult to 
	retrieve relevant articles efficiently.
	
	To address this, online portals commonly organize content under headings such as 
	“Political News,” “Sports News,” or “Entertainment News,” so that readers can navigate 
	the overwhelming flow of information. However, manually categorizing news is labor
	intensive and time-consuming, particularly as portals aggregate real-time news from 
	multiple sources. Therefore, developing an automated system to classify Nepali articles 
	into appropriate categories is both significant and timely.
	
	Automatic text classification refers to the process of assigning a class label to textual data 
	using computational techniques. In the news domain, such systems are crucial for 
	processing and categorizing massive amounts of information in real time. These 
	classification approaches can also be applied to other types of documents written in Nepali 
	text. 
	
	Implementing such systems for Nepali, however, poses unique challenges. The Nepali 
	language has a rich vocabulary and complex structure, necessitating adaptations to 
	universal text-processing methods. For instance, tokenization, punctuation removal, stop 
	word removal, and stemming must be tailored specifically for Nepali [2], [3]. 
	
	Recent advancements in transformer-based language models have significantly improved 
	text classification performance. The transformer architecture [5] introduced the self 
	attention mechanism, which allows models to capture contextual relationships between
	words effectively. For Nepali, models such as NepBERTa [2], Nepali Encoder 
	Transformers [3], and other adaptations of BERT [4], [1] have demonstrated promising 
	results for news classification. Additionally, deep neural networks (DNNs) have been 
	successfully applied for Nepali text document classification, showing their capability to 
	handle high-dimensional embeddings generated by transformer models [6].
	
	This project aims to develop an automated Nepali news classification system by combining 
	feature extraction using NepBERTa embeddings with a DNN classifier. The dataset 
	consists of Nepali news articles collected from public repositories such as Kaggle and 
	GitHub, segmented into training and testing subsets. The system converts input text into 
	numerical representations via feature extraction and leverages these embeddings to train a 
	model capable of categorizing news into predefined classes efficiently and accurately.
	\section{Problem Statement}
	As more and more people became online in Nepal and digital platforms become more 
	accessible and online consumption of news becomes more readily available for citizens 
	across the nation. Thus, it is expected that people turn to online news portals instead of 
	hardcopy newspapers. However, with such transition from printed newspaper to online 
	portals, there becomes more and more text-based information which needs to be classified 
	and categorized. 
	
	Unfortunately, classifying such unstructured data in Nepali manually is inefficient. It's a 
	redundant process, it's inconsistent over time with quality and it does not fulfill real-time 
	journalistic needs of the news portals with newspapers already in hand. Classification and 
	categorization need to happen quickly and accurately to ensure that readers are provided 
	with suitable articles.
	
	Thus, there is a critical need for a machine learning solution to automatically classify 
	Nepali news articles. This project aims to address the need for machine-based classification 
	of Nepali news articles.
