	\chapter{Literature Review}
	Automatic text classification plays a crucial role in natural language processing, particularly for applications such as recommendation systems, information retrieval, and media monitoring. In the context of news media, the massive volume of daily content makes manual categorization impractical. Traditional approaches, including rule-based methods and lexical feature-based classifiers such as TF-IDF combined with support vector machines (SVM) or Naïve Bayes, offer fast and interpretable solutions but struggle to capture long-range dependencies and contextual nuances inherent in natural language [1]–[3]. With the exponential growth of online Nepali news, reliable automatic classification systems are essential for improving content accessibility, reducing user effort, and enhancing recommendation quality.
	
	Over the past decade, NLP models have evolved significantly, transitioning from classical linear classifiers to deep learning architectures capable of understanding semantic context. Early methods such as SVM and Naïve Bayes rely on hand-crafted features, which are computationally efficient but limited in representing complex linguistic patterns. Recurrent neural networks (RNNs), particularly LSTM and GRU variants, address sequential dependencies by maintaining hidden states across tokens, thereby capturing variable-length inputs [4]. However, RNNs are inherently sequential in computation, which leads to slower training and inference times and challenges with vanishing gradients for long-range dependencies. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have also been applied to text classification by convolving over word embeddings to extract local features [5]. While CNNs are parallelizable and effective at local pattern recognition, they struggle with long-range dependencies. The transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. [6], addresses these limitations by relying entirely on self-attention mechanisms combined with positional encoding, allowing parallel processing of all tokens and efficient modeling of long-range dependencies. Transformers have since become the backbone of modern NLP architectures.
	
	BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) extends the transformer encoder to produce deep bidirectional contextual embeddings [2]. BERT is pre-trained using two main objectives: masked language modeling (MLM), which predicts randomly masked tokens, and next-sentence prediction (NSP), which classifies whether a given sentence follows another. The BERT-base model consists of 12 encoder layers, each with 768-dimensional hidden states and 12 self-attention heads. Fine-tuning BERT for downstream tasks typically requires only the addition of a simple linear classification layer on top of the pre-trained embeddings. BERT has achieved state-of-the-art results on multiple benchmarks, including GLUE and SQuAD, demonstrating its capacity to model complex contextual relationships in text. RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT) further improves upon BERT by removing the NSP objective, employing dynamic masking, using larger batch sizes, and adopting a byte-level BPE tokenizer [7]. These optimizations lead to consistently better performance on classification and other NLP tasks, making RoBERTa particularly suitable for robust language modeling.
	
	For Nepali NLP, several studies have adapted transformer-based models for news classification tasks. Kafle et al. [1] fine-tuned a multilingual BERT model on 71,970 Nepali news articles spanning 10 categories, achieving 93.3% accuracy and an F1 score of 0.936. Subsequent work by Kafle et al. [3] compared BERT and RoBERTa on the same dataset, reporting that RoBERTa outperformed BERT with an accuracy of 95.3% and an F1 score of 0.958. Sulav et al. [8] introduced NepBERTa, a BERT-based model pre-trained on a large Nepali corpus of 0.8 billion words from 36 news sites, achieving over 90% accuracy on several natural language understanding tasks such as named entity recognition (NER) and part-of-speech (POS) tagging. Maskey et al. [9] conducted a comparative analysis of auto-encoding transformers, including DistilBERT, DeBERTa, and XLM-RoBERTa, on 13 million tokens, highlighting trade-offs between speed and accuracy. These studies collectively demonstrate that transformer-based models, especially those adapted to Nepali text, significantly outperform traditional approaches such as SVMs, RNNs, and CNNs.
	
	Despite these advancements, several challenges remain in deploying transformer models for Nepali news classification. Full fine-tuning of large models such as BERT or RoBERTa is computationally expensive, often requiring significant hardware resources. Additionally, existing approaches typically fine-tune the entire transformer, which may be over-parameterized for classification tasks with a limited number of categories. The potential of frozen transformer embeddings as feature extractors, combined with lightweight classifiers, has not been fully explored in the Nepali context. Domain adaptation also remains a critical factor; while models such as NepBERTa provide strong general language representations, fine-tuning on news-specific corpora could further enhance classification accuracy with fewer trainable parameters.
	
	To address these gaps, this research proposes a hybrid architecture in which a pre-trained BERT or RoBERTa model is used as a frozen feature extractor, and the contextual embeddings are fed into a small deep neural network (DNN) for classification. This approach offers multiple advantages: it reduces training time by freezing the encoder, lowers memory requirements, and facilitates deployment on resource-constrained devices. By leveraging the high-quality representations provided by pre-trained transformers, the proposed method aims to maintain state-of-the-art accuracy while improving computational efficiency.
	
	In summary, the literature establishes that transformer-based models, including BERT and RoBERTa, provide superior performance for Nepali news classification compared to traditional machine learning approaches. Domain-specific adaptations, such as NepBERTa, further improve performance, highlighting the importance of pre-training on large Nepali corpora. The proposed use of a frozen transformer encoder with a lightweight DNN classifier addresses key limitations in computational efficiency and over-parameterization, offering a novel and practical approach for real-world Nepali news classification tasks.
	
