	\section{Tokenization}
	Tokenization is the process of breaking text into smaller units called tokens. Tokens can be
	words, subwords, or characters depending on the model.
	
	It is a crucial step in natural language processing because it transforms raw text into
	smaller, manageable units called tokens that a model can understand. NLP models,
	including BERT and NepBERTa, cannot directly process plain text, they require input in
	the form of tokens, which are then converted into numerical embeddings. It also allows the
	model to deal with rare or unseen words by breaking them into subwords, preventing outof-vocabulary issues.
	\subsection{Subword Tokenization strategy}
	Subword tokenization was applied to divide words into smaller meaningful units, allowing
	the model to handle rare or out-of-vocabulary (OOV) words effectively. This approach
	ensures that even previously unseen words can be represented through combinations of
	known subwords, improving the model’s ability to understand and classify Nepali news
	text accurately.
	
	Original sentence:\\
	\nepalifont "नेपाल एक सुन्त्दर देश हो।"
	
	Tokanized:\\
	\nepalifont "नेपाल, एक, सु\#\# न्त्दर,देश,हो।" \\
	Here, \#\# indicates a continuation of a subword from the previous token.
	\normalfont
	\subsection{Padding and Truncation}
	Padding is the process of adding extra tokens to make all input sequences the same length.
	NLP models require input sequences in a fixed size because they process data in batches.
	Since sentences naturally have different lengths, shorter sentences are “padded” with
	special [PAD] tokens to match the maximum sequence length in the batch.
	[PAD] is ignored during model processing using an attention mask.\\
	Example:
	
	\nepalifont काठमाडौँराजधानी हो।\\
	Let, the maximum length in the batch is 5
	
	Tokanized:\\
	\nepalifont ['काठमाड ौँ', 'राजधानी', 'हो', '।']
	
	After Padding:\\
	\nepalifont['काठमाड ौँ', 'राजधानी', 'हो', '।', '[PAD]'
	\normalfont
	\section{Model Architecture}
	A pre-trained transformer model is used with a classification head (a fully connected layer
	followed by softmax).
	
	The model consists of two main components: the NepBERTa feature extractor and a
	Dense Neural Network classifier. NepBERTa first transforms the input Nepali text into a
	fixed-size dense vector. This vector is then passed to a fully connected dense layer,
	followed by a softmax activation function that gives output of probabilities for each
	predefined category. The softmax layer ensures that the sum of all predicted class
	probabilities equals one, enabling clear classification decisions. This end-to-end
	architecture allows the model to learn both linguistic features and decision boundaries in a
	single pipeline. 
	\subsection{NepBERTa}
	NepBERTa is a transformer-based language model trained specifically on Nepali which is
	biased on RoBERTa Model. RoBERTa is a transformer-based deep learning model that
	learns contextual representations of text by looking at both left and right context
	simultaneously. In our system, NepBERTa serves as the feature extractor, transforming
	raw Nepali news text into high-dimensional dense vectors (typically of size 768). These
	embeddings capture the semantic meaning of the entire text, taking into account grammar,
	syntax, and contextual relationships between words.
	
	Example:
	
	\nepalifont Input:"प्रधानमन्त्रीलेसम्बोधनगरे "\\
	Output: 768-dimensional contextual vector (numeric)
