	\subsection{Transformer Architecture Basics}
	\begin{enumerate}
		\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{\roman{enumi}.}
		\item Encoder Structure\\
		The transformer encoder consists of stacked layers, each with two main
		components:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Self-Attention Mechanism\\
			Self-attention allows each token to attend to all other tokens in a sequence.\\
			The steps are:\\
			Compute Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V) vectors for each token using
			learnable weight matrices:
			\begin{eqnarray}
				Q = X W^Q \\
				K = X W^K \\
				V = X W^K
			\end{eqnarray}
			where:\\
			X is the input matrix of n tokens
			$W^Q$,$W^K$,$W^V$ are learnable parameters
			\begin{figure}[H]
				\centering
				\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{"nepberta file/self-attention-matrix-calculation"}
				\caption[Attention Matrix]{Attention Matrix}
				\label{fig:self-attention-matrix-calculation}
				\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
				\small Source: \url{https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/}
			\end{figure}
			\item Feed-Forward Network (FFN)\\
			Each encoder layer also contains a position-wise FFN applied to each token
			independently:
			\begin{align}
				\mathrm{FFN}(x) &= \mathrm{ReLU}(x W_1 + b_1) W_2 + b_2
			\end{align}
			where W1, W2 and b1, b2 are learnable parameters.
		\end{enumerate}
		\item Positional Encoding\\
		Transformers do not process tokens sequentially like RNNs, so positional
		information must be added to input embeddings to indicate the order of words in a sentence. This is done using Positional Encoding (PE).\\
		For each position pos and embedding dimension i, the positional encoding is
		defined as:
		\begin{align}
			PE_{(POS,2i)} &= \sin(\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/dmodel}}) \\
			PE_{(POS,2i+1)} &= \cos(\frac{pos}{10000^{2i/dmodel}})
		\end{align}
		\textbf{How it works?}\\
		Even dimensions use sine, odd dimensions use cosine. Each position gets a unique
		combination of sinusoids, allowing the model to distinguish token positions.
		Positional encodings are added to token embeddings before entering the encoder.
		This ensures that the transformer knows the order of tokens while still allowing
		parallel processing.
		\item Token Embeddings\\
		Token embeddings represent each input token as a dense numerical vector that
		captures its semantic meaning. In NepBERTa, text is first split into subword
		tokens using a subword tokenization strategy, and each token is mapped to a
		fixed-dimensional embedding vector.
		\\
		\item Contextual Embeddings\\
		Contextual embeddings represent tokens in a sentence by incorporating
		information from surrounding tokens, allowing the same word to have different
		representations depending on its context. In NepBERTa, contextual embeddings
		are produced by passing token embeddings through multiple transformer
		encoder layers.\\ 
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\subsection{Embeddings}
	Embeddings are numerical representations of data, such as words, sentences, or images, that capture their meaning in a mathematical form. By converting raw data into vectors, embeddings allow computers to understand similarity and relationships items with similar meanings have vectors that are close to each other. They are widely used in machine learning tasks like search, recommendation systems, clustering, and natural language processing because they help models work with complex, unstructured data more effectively.\\
	\begin{enumerate}
		\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{\roman{enumi}.}
		\item How Embeddings are generated?\\
		Step 1: Tokenization:\\
		The input text is split into tokens or subwords.\\
		Input: \nepalifont"नेपाल सुन्दर देश हो।"\\
		Tokenized: \nepalifont['नेपाल', 'सु'\#\#न्दर', 'देश', 'हो', '।']\\
		
		Step 2: Token IDs\\
		Each token is mapped to a unique integer ID from the model’s vocabulary.\\
		\nepalifont['नेपाल', 'सु','\#\#न्त्दर', 'देश', 'हो', '।']\\
		->[101, 1234, 5678, 2345, 3456, 102]\\
		
		Token IDs are fixed for known tokens i.e. If a word (or subword) is already in
		NepBERTa’s vocabulary, it always maps to the same token ID. If the word is not in
		the vocabulary, it will be split into subwords, each with its own ID. so original word
		does not have a single ID, but the sequence of subword IDs.\\
		
		Step 3: Embedding Lookup\\
		The model has a embedding matrix (size: vocabulary × embedding dimension). For
		each token ID, we pick the corresponding row from the embedding matrix and this
		process is called embedding lookup. Words that are not in the model’s vocabulary
		are splitted into smaller subwords that are in the vocabulary.\\
		
		Step 4: Positional Embedding\\
		Transformers like BERT or NepBERTa cannot inherently understand the order of
		words in a sentence because they process all tokens in parallel. Positional Encoding
		is a way to inject information about token positions into the embeddings. It tells the
		model “this token is first, this one is second, etc.”, which is crucial for
		understanding sentence structure and meaning.\\
		
		Final embedding = Token embedding + Positional embedding\\
		
		For Example:\\
		"नेपाल राजधानी हो" and "राजधानी नेपाल हो" have the same words but different meanings.\\
		
		In above example:\\
		Even though both sentences have the same tokens, the positional embeddings are
		different because the order is different, so model can understand that “नेपाल राजधानी हो”
		$\neq$ “राजधानी नेपाल हो”.\\
		\begin{figure}[H]
			\centering
			\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{nepberta file/transformer_positional_encoding_example.png}
			\caption{Positional Encoding}
			\label{fig:transformer-positional-encoding-example}
			\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
			\small Source: \url{https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-bert/}
		\end{figure}
		Step 5: Contextualization via Transformer Layers\\
		After token embeddings are combined with positional embeddings, the model
		passes them through transformer layers. Contextualization means that each token’s
		embedding is adjusted based on the other tokens in the sentence. Thus, the final
		embedding of a token captures not just its meaning, but also its meaning in context.\\
		
		Example: Sentence "नेपाल राजधानी हो"\\
		Token Initial Embedding (e+p)\\
		After Contextualization (final embedding)\\
		नेपाल \hspace{2.4cm}e0+p0 = e0’ (adjusted based on “राजधानी” \& “हो”)\\
		राजधानी \hspace{2cm}e1+p1 = e1’ (adjusted based on “नेपाल” \& “हो”)\\
		हो \hspace{2.9cm}	e2+p2 = e2’(adjusted based “नेपाल”\& “राजधानी”)\\
		As the embedding is depend on other token in the sentence one word can have two
		different embeddings in two different sentences.\\
		
		Step 6: Output Embeddings\\
		Output embeddings are the final contextual vector representations of each token\\
		\item Benefits of Embedding over Traditional Feature Extraction:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Understands Meaning (Semantic Understanding).
			\item Traditional methods create huge vectors (up to 10,000+ dimensions).
			\item Embeddings use small dense vectors (768 dimensions).
			\item Same word can have different vectors depending on the sentence.
			\item Embeddings show closeness between related words:\\
			“नेपाल” → “काठमाड ौँ” closer\\
			“रोग” → “स्वास््य” closer
			\item Models like BERT/NepBERTa already learned from large datasets.
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{enumerate}
	\subsection{Dense Neural Network Classifier}
	On top of the embeddings generated by NepBERTa, we added a simple feedforward neural
	network (MLP), also known as a Dense layer, followed by a softmax activation function.
	This layer serves as the classifier that maps the semantic vector to a fixed number of output
	categories (e.g., Politics, Sports, Business, Entertainment, etc.)
	
	Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) consists of fully connected dense layers that transform
	input data from one dimension to another. It is called multi-layer because it contains an
	input layer, one or more hidden layers and an output layer. The purpose of an MLP is to
	model complex relationships between inputs and outputs
	\begin{enumerate}
		\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{\roman{enumi}.}
		\item Architecture of MLP\\
		The Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) is used as the final classification component in
		the proposed system. It takes the contextual embeddings generated by NepBERTa
		as input and maps them to predefined news categories.
		\begin{figure}[H]
			\centering
			\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{photos/images}
			\caption[MLP Architecture]{MLP Architecture}
			\label{fig:MLP-Diagram}
			\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
			\small Source: \url{https://tinyurl.com/adakhjya}
		\end{figure}
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item Input Layer\\
			Receives the sentence-level embedding (e.g., the [CLS] token embedding)
			produced by NepBERTa. The input dimension is equal to embeddings size.
			\item Hidden Layers\\
			There are One or more fully connected (dense) layers
			Each applies a linear transformation function followed by linear
			transformation funtion.
			These layers learn discriminative patterns from the extracted features.
			\item Output Layer\\
			A fully connected layer with neurons equal to the number of news
			categories is output layer.
			It uses the Softmax activation function to produce class probability
			distributions
		\end{enumerate}
		\item Activation Function\\
		Activation functions introduce non-linearity into the Multi-Layer Perceptron
		(MLP), enabling the network to learn complex patterns from the input features. In
		this project, different activation functions are used at different layers of the network.
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit):\\
			ReLU is applied in the hidden layers of the MLP to allow efficient training
			and mitigate the vanishing gradient problem. It is defined as:
			\begin{align}
				\mathrm{ReLU}(x)=\mathrm{Max}(0,x)
			\end{align}
			\begin{figure}[H]
				\centering
				\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{photos/screenshot001}
				\caption[ReLU]{ReLU Activation Function}
				\label{fig:screenshot001}
				\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
				\small Source: \url{https://tinyurl.com/5998a3vj}
			\end{figure}
			
			\item Softmax:\\
			The Softmax function is used in the output layer to convert the network’s
			outputs into a probability distribution over the news categories:
			\begin{align}
				softmax=\frac{e^{z_i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{c}e^j}
			\end{align}
			where:\\
			C is the number of classes. The class with the highest probability is selected as the predicted category.
			\\
			\\
			\\
			\begin{figure}[H]
				\centering
				\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{photos/softmax}
				\caption[Softmax]{Softmax Function Graph}
				\label{fig:softmax}
				\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
				\small Source: \url{https://l1nq.com/rbRRo}
			\end{figure}
			
		\end{enumerate}
		
		\item Forward Propagation\\
		Forward propagation describes the process by which input features pass through
		the layers of the Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) to produce an output prediction.
		In this project, the MLP receives contextual embeddings generated by NepBERTa
		and processes them through a sequence of fully connected layers.
		Given an input embedding vector x, the output of each hidden layer is computed
		as:
		\begin{align}
			H = \mathrm{f}(W * h + b)
		\end{align}
		where:\\
		W and b are the weight matrix and bias vector of layer l.
		F(⋅)is the activation function (ReLU)
		After passing through the hidden layers, the output layer computes class
		probabilities using the Softmax function.
		\\
		\\
		\\
		\begin{figure}[H]
			\centering
			\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{"photos/forward propagation"}
			\caption[Forward Propagation]{Forward Propagation}
			\label{fig:forward_propagation}
			\vspace{0.5em} % optional space before source
			\small Source: \url{https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/23/4718}
		\end{figure}
		\item Loss Function\\
		The loss function measures the difference between the predicted class probabilities
		and the true class labels during model training. For the multi-class news
		classification task, categorical cross-entropy loss is used, as it is well suited for
		classification problems with more than two classes.
		Given the true label y and the predicted probability distribution the loss is defined
		as:
		\begin{align}
			L=\sum_{i=1}^{c}y_i\log(y_i)
		\end{align}
		\item Backward propagation\\
		Backward propagation (backpropagation) is the algorithm used to train neural networks by minimizing prediction error. After a forward pass, the error is computed using a loss function, for example Mean Squared Error (MSE)
		\begin{align}
			L=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(y_i - \hat{y_i})^2
		\end{align}
		The algorithm then applies the chain rule to propagate this error backward through the network and compute gradients. For a neuron, the weighted sum is
		\begin{align}
			z=\mathrm{w} \cdot \mathrm{x}+b
		\end{align}
		and the output after applying an activation function f is
		\begin{align}
			a=\mathrm{f}(z)
		\end{align}
		The gradient of the loss with respect to the weights is
		\begin{align}
			\frac{\partial L}{\partial W}=\dfrac{\partial L}{\partial a} \cdot \frac{\partial a}{\partial z}\cdot \frac{\partial z}{\partial w}
		\end{align}
		Finally, the weights are updated using gradient descent:
		\begin{align}
			w_{new}=w_{old}-\eta	\frac{\partial L}{\partial W}
		\end{align}
		where:\\ 
		$\eta$ is the learning rate. This process is repeated for many iterations until the loss is minimized and the network learns meaningful patterns from the data.
		\begin{figure}[H]
			\centering
			\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{photos/screenshot002}
			\caption[Backward Propagation]{Backward-propagation}
			\label{fig:backward-propagation}
			\vspace{0.5em} % optional: adds a small space before the source
			\small Source: \url{https://sl1nk.com/7UGYE}
		\end{figure}
		
	\end{enumerate}
	
